
DDL — Data Definition Language

DDL (Data Definition Language) is used to define, create, modify, and remove the structure of database objects such as databases, tables, 
columns, constraints, views, and indexes.

🎯 Main DDL Commands

🧩 Command	 📌 Purpose
🟢 CREATE:   Creates a new database object
🟡 ALTER:   	Modifies an existing database object
🔴 DROP:	    Permanently removes a database object
🟠 TRUNCATE:	Removes all rows from a table while keeping its structure
🔵 RENAME:	  Renames an existing database object

📚 DDL Overview

🟢 CREATE

Used to create new database objects such as:

🗄️ Database
📋 Table
👁️ View
🔍 Index
🗂️ Schema

🟡 ALTER

Used to modify the structure of an existing database object.

Examples:

➕ Add a column
✏️ Modify a column
🔄 Rename a column
➕ Add a constraint
❌ Drop a column

🔴 DROP

Used to permanently delete a database object.

Examples:

🗑️ Drop database
🗑️ Drop table
🗑️ Drop view
🗑️ Drop index

🟠 TRUNCATE

Used to remove all records from a table while keeping the table structure.

🔵 RENAME

Used to change the name of an existing database object.

